WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:08.040
In December 1964, an expedition worked on Rapa Nui.

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.020
One member, Georges Nogrady, collected soil.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.620
In Montreal, a bacterium was found to kill fungi.

4
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:23.840
In 1972, Suren Sehgal isolated the compound and named it rapamycin.

5
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.480
But why does it also stop cells from growing?

6
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:33.913
In Michael Hall's laboratory, Joseph Heitman spread yeast on plates containing

7
00:00:33.913 --> 00:00:37.573
rapamycin, and kept the rare mutants that grew anyway.

8
00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:44.853
Most had mutations in the gene for FPR1, the yeast version of FKBP12.

9
00:00:45.713 --> 00:00:48.053
So rapamycin does nothing alone.

10
00:00:48.473 --> 00:00:51.073
It must first bind FKBP12.

11
00:00:51.753 --> 00:00:53.093
The pair is the poison.

12
00:00:54.017 --> 00:01:02.517
The remaining mutants had changes in one of two new genes, TOR1 and TOR2, for

13
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:04.037
target of rapamycin.

14
00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:07.117
The screen was published in 1991.

15
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:14.637
In 1994 and 1995, several laboratories found the mammalian protein the pair binds.

16
00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:16.797
Its name is mTOR.

17
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:19.917
mTOR is a large kinase.

18
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:24.617
Beside its kinase domain is a small domain called FRB.

19
00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:31.877
Rapamycin sits between FKBP12 and FRB like glue between two blocks.

20
00:01:32.897 --> 00:01:37.377
The bound FKBP12 narrows the substrates' path to the active site.

21
00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:40.297
The drug does not compete with ATP.

22
00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:44.717
It is allosteric: it acts from a site other than the active site.
